# A device that is merely BUSY must not look dead to its partner (docs/pageflip.md section 4),
# LEFT half: it holds the render lock for ten seconds in the middle of a healthy pair.
#
# On real e-ink that is not a contrivance. An image-bearing page was measured at 4,241 ms of render
# on hardware against 0.88 s for a text page, and two of those back to back exceed the peer's
# four-missed-heartbeat timeout. Here a refresh is microseconds, so the window has to be asked for:
# CROSSPOINT_SIM_RENDER_STALL_MS, set by the harness, makes one refresh take that long.
#
# This half presses nothing. The stall is triggered by the render that the RIGHT half's page turn
# causes here, which is what makes the timing a consequence of the pair rather than of this script.
#
# It also quits FIRST, a few seconds ahead of the right half. Leaving last would put this instance's
# own departure inside the right half's log as a second silence, and the check that matters there is
# that the right half never gave this device up -- a check that cannot tell a stall from a shutdown.
#
# The waits below are absolute, not relative to the pairing: two instances started together pair in
# about two seconds here, and the stall is armed on uptime.
log pageflip-renderstall-left: start
expect_timeout 90000
expect Entering activity: Home
wait 800
tap confirm
expect Entering activity: EpubReader
expect peer present
# Everything after this belongs to the stall: the harness arms it for the first refresh past sixteen
# seconds of uptime, which is the render caused by the right half's first press at about eighteen.
#
# `wait` rather than an expect on the release line: ScriptDriver matches expects against a 256-line
# ring, and a page render buries anything logged next to it.
wait 32000
screenshot /screenshots/pf-rs-left-00.bmp
wait 1000
log pageflip-renderstall-left: done
quit
